Two geoframes, coarsest first: \code{country} (36) and \code{bus} (41 model regions,
the atoms). Five countries — DK, ES, FR, GB, IT — are split into two buses
each, which is how 36 becomes 41.

PyPSA's synchronous zones are carried as a \code{sync} \strong{column}, deliberately
not as a geoframe. \code{sync} is a genuine partition of the atoms and is coarser
than \code{country} by member count (7 against 36), but it does \strong{not nest}
inside it: those same five countries each straddle two zones. A geoframe
chain must nest — listing \code{sync} in it made those countries children of two
parents, and the spatial roll-up would then add each one's balance into both
zones. Group by the column, or build a separate geoscale from it, when
synchronous zones are what you want.
